  "identifier": "NG3536",
  "full_title": "The Madonna and Child with Saints John the Baptist, Elizabeth and Catherine of Alexandria",
  "short_title": "The Madonna and Child with Saints",
  "object_name": "Picture",
  "style": "high Renaissance",
  "material": "oil on canvas",
  "dimensions": "73 × 119.4 cm",
  "production_date": "probably 1530-5",
  "brief_description": "Saint Catherine sits reading a book  on the left, beside the broken spiked wheel on which she was tortured during her martyrdom. The Virgin Mary's elderly cousin Saint Elizabeth sits on the right holding her son, the infant John the Baptist. He wears his traditional camel skin and holds a scroll that reads 'Ecce Agnus Dei' ('Behold the Lamb of God'), signifying Christ’s future sacrifice for humankind. The Christ Child turns on his mother’s lap to look at Saint Catherine, who gazes out of the picture at us. There are some traces of scribbled writing on the pier behind Saint Catherine but it is not clear what, if anything, they mean.\n\nThe poor quality of the painting suggests that Bonifazio was not personally involved in making the picture; it may have been put together by a follower from a combination of motifs from Bonifazio’s work. The costumes can be dated to the early 1530s.",
